Summary Report: Identification of the show and the third line in its theme song, with full working history, evidence, and uncertainties 1) Original question recap - Find a show whose premise features undercover characters using martial arts and technology to protect people from an evil character. - It originally aired between 2005 and 2012, had fewer than 30 episodes as of 2023, and was produced by a company founded in the 1900s that faced a resource shortage due to suppliers shutting down by 2023. - The show also had a web game as of 2016. - Determine the third line in the show’s theme song. - Provide a comprehensive report including candidate shows, evidence, reasoning, and any plausible alternative answers depending on interpretation. 2) Approach and plan - Step 1: Compile a shortlist of candidate shows that match the clues and gather citations for premise, years, episode count, producers/founding year, 2023 supplier-shutdown issue, and evidence of a web game around 2016. - Step 2: For the strongest candidate, locate primary-source opening theme videos, transcribe lyrics from at least two independent English uploads, define a counting rule for “third line,” identify the third line, and note any version differences or uncertainties. Cross-check other candidates’ themes (lyrical vs instrumental). - Step 3: Compile the evidence, document uncertainties, and present the final answer plus any plausible alternatives. 3) Working history and tool usage - Tool Call 1: Internet-capable agent tasked to identify candidate shows matching all clues and compile a structured shortlist with citations. • Objective: Title, years, episodes, premise fit, villain, production company with founding year, 2023 supplier-shutdown resource shortage evidence, web game circa 2016 evidence, and early leads on theme song for at least six candidates. • Result summary: – Strongest candidate: Chop Socky Chooks (2007–2008; 26 episodes). Fits martial arts/tech vs central villain (Dr. Wasabi), within date range, under 30 episodes; produced by Aardman Animations (founded 1972). Unique fit for 2023 resource shortage due to Aardman’s clay supplier (Newclay) shutdown; web game evidence unverified via archives. Sources: ▪ Chop Socky Chooks: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chop_Socky_Chooks ▪ Aardman Animations and supplier issue: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aardman_Animations ▪ Telegraph coverage: https://www.telegraph.co.uk/films/2023/11/17/crisis-looms-for-chicken-run-aardman-clay-shortage/ ▪ Deadline coverage: https://deadline.com/2023/11/wallace-and-gromit-aardman-denies-clay-running-out-1235628760/ ▪ A.V. Club: https://www.avclub.com/chicken-run-studio-aardman-is-apparently-running-out-of-1851033934 – Additional candidates (meet most criteria but lack the 2023 supplier-shutdown evidence or have partial premise mismatches): Sym-Bionic Titan (2010–2011; 20 eps), ThunderCats (2011; 26 eps), Human Target (2010–2011; 25 eps), The Cape (2011; 10 eps), Samurai Girl (2008; 6 eps), Guyver: The Bioboosted Armor (2005–2006; 26 eps). Web game evidence unverified for these; instrumental or non-lyrical openings common among them. Sources: ▪ Sym-Bionic Titan: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sym-Bionic_Titan ▪ ThunderCats (2011): https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ThunderCats_(2011_TV_series) ▪ Human Target (2010): https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Human_Target_(2010_TV_series) ▪ The Cape (2011): https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Cape_(2011_TV_series) ▪ Samurai Girl: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Samurai_Girl ▪ Guyver (2005): https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guyver:_The_Bioboosted_Armor_(2005_TV_series) – Web game (2016) archival attempts: Multiple Wayback searches on Cartoon Network domains returned no captures (CN game pages often blocked). Therefore (f) remains unverified. - Tool Call 2: Internet-capable agent tasked to find primary-source English opening theme videos for Chop Socky Chooks, produce line-by-line lyric transcription from at least two independent uploads, define counting rule for “third line,” identify third line, document version differences, and cross-check other candidates’ theme types. • Objective: Obtain authoritative opening videos, normalize lyrics, define counting method, and determine the third line. Also check whether other candidates have lyrical openings. • Result summary: – Primary English opening videos found: ▪ “Chop Socky Chooks | Opening Theme (English) (HD)” (0:52) — includes fan-supplied lyric text in description/pinned comment: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iEhELU-mXfU ▪ “Chop Socky Chooks - Theme Song & End Credits (2007) [Cartoon Network UK airing]” (off-air UK): https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=l2Y6Tw8XWjQ ▪ Supporting regional structure confirmation (Poland): https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tNA_U-ostpU ▪ Additional English short clip: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mTbLaq3Sq7I – Normalized English lyric sequence (narrative lines with chants noted): ▪ Intro chant: “Ba-Da, Ba-Da, Ba-Da” ▪ Line 1 (narrative): “Evil fish Wasabi is cooking up trouble” ▪ Brief chant: “Ba-Da, Ba-Da, Ba-Da” ▪ Line 2 (narrative): “Kung-Fu chickens get there on the double” ▪ Chorus burst (chant): “Ch-ch-ch-chop, Chop Socky Chooks” ▪ Chant: “Bang, bang, bang, bang” ▪ Line 3 (narrative): “Bubba and the Ninja Chimps are ready and rockin’” ▪ Line 4 (narrative): “Choppin’ and the Sockin’, the Chooks are gonna stop ’em” ▪ Chant: “Hey, hey, hey, hey” (some texts list five “Hey”s) ▪ Line 5 (narrative): “Chick P, Chuckie Chan, and K.O. Joe” ▪ Line 6 (narrative): “Three superheroes get ready to” ▪ Chant: “Go, go, go, go” ▪ Line 7 (narrative): “Gonna stop Wasabi and his crazy schemes” ▪ Line 8 (narrative): “Here comes the kickin’, choppin’ Chop Socky Team” ▪ Closing chants vary; do not affect the third-line determination. – Counting rule adopted: ▪ Count only distinct lead-melody phrases with lexical content (words) as narrative lines; exclude non-lexical chants like “Ba-Da,” “Ch-ch-ch-chop,” “Bang,” “Hey,” “Go” unless they function as standalone lead statements. ▪ Rationale: “Line” reasonably refers to the sung lyric phrases that convey the synopsis, not percussive/onomatopoeic chant fragments. – Third line under the adopted rule: ▪ “Bubba and the Ninja Chimps are ready and rockin’” – Alternative plausible third-line answers (if chants counted as lines or different segmentation used): ▪ “Kung-Fu chickens get there on the double” ▪ “Ch-ch-ch-chop, Chop Socky Chooks” ▪ “Ba-Da, Ba-Da, Ba-Da” – Official lyric publication: None found (no press kit/soundtrack booklet/closed-caption lyric transcript). Lyrics appear fan-transcribed, but multiple uploads align on narrative sequence. – Cross-check of other candidates’ openings: ▪ Sym-Bionic Titan: instrumental (non-lyrical). ▪ ThunderCats (2011): instrumental (non-lyrical). ▪ Human Target (2010): instrumental. ▪ The Cape (2011): instrumental. ▪ Samurai Girl (2008): no consistent lyrical main title. ▪ Guyver (2005): lyrical Japanese opening (non-English). 4) Identification of the show - Chop Socky Chooks best satisfies the combined criteria: • Undercover martial-arts heroes using gadgets to protect mall citizens from a central evil character (Dr. Wasabi). • Aired in 2007–2008 (within 2005–2012). • 26 episodes (<30). • Produced by Aardman Animations (founded 1972). • 2023 resource-shortage issue: Newclay Products (Aardman’s clay supplier) shut down in March 2023, widely reported; Aardman later clarified alternative sourcing. – Wikipedia: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aardman_Animations – Telegraph: https://www.telegraph.co.uk/films/2023/11/17/crisis-looms-for-chicken-run-aardman-clay-shortage/ – Deadline: https://deadline.com/2023/11/wallace-and-gromit-aardman-denies-clay-running-out-1235628760/ • Web game as of 2016: Unverified via Wayback due to Cartoon Network archival limitations (common for CN game pages). Not disproven. 5) Key facts and quotes relevant to the task - Chop Socky Chooks premise: “The show is about a trio of kung fu fighting chickens who live and work in a city-sized shopping mall owned by their archenemy, Dr. Wasabi.” Source: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chop_Socky_Chooks#Premise - Episodes: 26. Source: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chop_Socky_Chooks - Opening theme: “Chop Socky Chooks”; ending theme instrumental; theme credited to Eggplant. Source: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chop_Socky_Chooks - Opening theme videos used: ▪ https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iEhELU-mXfU ▪ https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=l2Y6Tw8XWjQ ▪ https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mTbLaq3Sq7I 6) Uncertainties and limitations - No official lyric sheet located; lyrics are fan-transcribed. However, multiple uploads align on narrative sequence. - Minor chant-count discrepancies (e.g., number of “Hey”s) across uploads; they do not affect the identified third narrative line. - Exact timestamps were not machine-extracted due to tool timeouts; the line order is based on cross-verified audio. - Web game (2016) remains unverified via archives; Cartoon Network domains often block Wayback captures for interactive pages. 7) Final answer determination - Under a clear, reasonable counting rule that includes only lexical narrative lines and excludes chants, the third line in the Chop Socky Chooks theme song is: • “Bubba and the Ninja Chimps are ready and rockin’” - Alternative plausible third-line answers (depending on different counting interpretations) are documented for completeness: • “Kung-Fu chickens get there on the double” • “Ch-ch-ch-chop, Chop Socky Chooks” • “Ba-Da, Ba-Da, Ba-Da” Final Answer: \boxed{Bubba and the Ninja Chimps are ready and rockin’}
